\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{How to build the tree: neighbour joining}{55}{figure.caption.34}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.8}{\ignorespaces  Different graphical representations of evolutionary trees. \textbf  {Left: a Phylogram} In these representations, the \textit  {length of the edges matters}, because it indicates \textit  {genetic distance} (mutations) and therefore, although not precisely, \textit  {time}. In this example, these numbers (like 0.226) likely represent the number of expected substitutions per amino acid site. A higher number of mutations means that more time has passed. \textbf  {Right: a Cladogram} here edge length does not contain any information. You can see the difference from the phylogram, because here all the leaves line up vertically the right side.}}{56}{figure.caption.34}\protected@file@percent }
